--------------------------------

See file addons/README_basefunctions


Numerical integration
---------------------

See file addons/README_Gauss


Procedure pointers
------------------

Summary of assignment of user functions/subroutines to procedure pointers:

  coefficients%vfunc => velocityvfunc

NOTES:

 1) The user functions/subroutines only need to be assigned if they are
    actually being used. If a required assignment is forgotten an error message
    with be given.
 2) The user is free to choose the functions/subroutines for assignment as
    long as the interface is correct.


List of the coefficients
------------------------

1   numerical integration rule for the internal domain integrals of elements.

2   The vector coefficient u (=velocity) is as follows:
     0: constant, given by coefficients%r(1:3)
     1: given by a vector function, vfuncnr=coefficient%i(3)
        NOTE: vector function must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc(1)%p
     2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(1)
     3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)
    NOTE: the number of velocity components is ndim+1, where the first ndim
    components represent the "convection" and the last component the right-hand
    side of the height function equation.

3   vfuncnr for the coefficient u (=velocity)

4   Method (method) for spatial discretization:
     0: standard Galerkin
     1: SUPG.

5   Time integration scheme (timeint):
    1: first-order, implicit Euler
    2: second-order, implicit Gear

6   interpolation of the position of the interface
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral interpolation also the polynomial order p
          needs to be set with coefficients%i(7).

7   Polynomial order p of the spectral/high-order interpolation for the
    position

8   nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(1) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by
        coefficients%i(1) to each subdomain.

9   Type of integration (inttype):
      0: standard Gauss-Legendre (analytical expressions)
      1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
      2: mapped Gauss (quad->triangle).
      3: standard Gauss-Legendre (numerical tables)

10  Interpolation of the velocity on the interface for coefficients%i(2)=2:
      0: interpolation is the same as for the position (coefficients%i(6))
    >=1: independent interpolation for the velocity.
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral, also the polynomial order p needs to be
          set with coefficients%i(11).

11  Polynomial order p of the high-order or spectral interpolation for the
    velocity.

12  Shape of the element:
    0 Use the interpolation of the position (coefficients%i(6))
    1 Use the interpolation of the velocity (coefficients%i(10))

13  Specifies how the integration rules must be set:
    0: default, i.e. direcly with the value in coefficients.
    1: by specifying the minimal polynomial order that the rule must be able
       to integrate exactly.
    Note, that the value of coefficients%i(13) is overruled by setting
    SET_GAUSS_BY_ORDER = .true. in the module limits_m.

14  Check for zero velocity and avoid dividing by zero in SUPG.
      0: no
      1: yes
    If a zero velocity is detected in ANY of the integration points of an
    element, the method is switched to using htype=3 and Uscaling=4 with 
    Uglobal = coefficient%r(6) in the velocity scaling for ALL integration
    points.
    NOTE: this is a brute force approach to avoid divide by zero, when
          the velocity is exactly zero. It should be used only for that case
          and normally be off.
    NOTE: htype=3 means h is determined by the size of the element:
            2D: h = (elementsize)**0.5
            3D: h = (elementsize)**1/3 (not applicable here)
          hence: h is a constant per element
    NOTE: Uglobal = coefficient%r(6), used in Uscaling=4, must be set to
          a positive value.

15--100 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.
